PEMROGRAMAN LINEAR SOLUTION
==========================

PRODUCT OVERVIEW
----------------
Application name : PEMROGRAMAN LINEAR SOLUTION
Developer        : ALFANDOXEON
GitHub           : ALFANDOXEON
Language         : PYTHON
Interface        : INTERACTIVE CLI BUILT WITH RICH
Application UI   : ENGLISH
Architecture     : MVC AND OBJECT-ORIENTED DESIGN

PURPOSE
-------
An interactive command-line application for defining, solving, and reviewing
linear programming problems commonly encountered in coursework.

CORE USER EXPERIENCE
--------------------
- Guide users through entering an objective function, constraints, and variable
  domains. Users choose the number and names of variables and constraints and
  enter all coefficients and right-hand-side values themselves.
- Support maximization and minimization, and <=, >=, and = constraints.
- Start with non-negative variables as the default; make variable domains and
  bounds explicit and configurable rather than silently assuming them.
- Show a clear summary of the model and let users confirm or edit it before
  solving.
- Report the result, status, and useful solving steps. Clearly distinguish
  optimal, infeasible, unbounded, and numerically inconclusive problems.
- Allow users to solve another problem or return to the main menu without
  restarting the application.

CLI DESIGN
----------
- Use RICH for styled panels, tables, prompts, progress/status messages, and
  terminal-aware layout.
- Use uppercase for all application-owned visible text. Preserve user-entered
  mathematical labels and values where changing them would alter the model.
- Do not use emoji.
- Keep an application-and-developer header and a footer/navigation area visible
  in each screen. Clear/redraw the terminal when moving between screens.
- Adapt layout to terminal width; avoid relying on fixed-width output.
- Keep colors decorative: statuses and instructions must remain understandable
  without color.

ENGINEERING REQUIREMENTS
------------------------
- Use Python with an MVC structure and object-oriented domain types.
- Separate presentation, input validation, problem representation, solving,
  and result formatting.
- Validate every user-controlled input, including counts, names, numeric values,
  operators, and variable domains. Give actionable English error messages and
  allow correction without losing previously entered data.
- Handle invalid, incomplete, infeasible, unbounded, and numerically difficult
  models explicitly. Do not report a successful solution when none was found.
- Keep the solver independent of the CLI so it can be tested directly.

INITIAL SCOPE
-------------
- Define one linear objective with a user-selected direction.
- Define a configurable number of linear constraints and decision variables.
- Solve supported models with a documented simplex-based method; account for
  mixed constraint directions and equality constraints.
- Present the optimal objective value and variable values, plus a concise
  explanation of the outcome.

OUT OF SCOPE FOR THE FIRST RELEASE
----------------------------------
- Graphical interface, file/database persistence, and nonlinear or integer
  programming.
- Custom finite variable bounds.
- Claims of exact arithmetic or support for every numerically ill-conditioned
  model. Numerical limits and solver status must be documented.
